{"entries":{"chapter-8-study-principles-of-geriatric-assessment|Chapter 8 Study: Principles of Geriatric Assessment":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Extend disease-focused assessment to cognition, mood, function, frailty, social support, finances, environment, spirituality and care preferences (p117).":["u3"],"copy|Instruments support rather than replace clinical judgment. Individual clinician assessment and interdisciplinary comprehensive geriatric assessment (CGA) are related but distinct (p117).":["u4"],"copy|Tailor assessment to the setting: home visits reveal environmental risks; nursing-home assessment emphasizes nutrition and self-care rather than shopping or meal preparation (p118).":["u5"],"copy|Weight loss above 4% in the previous year prompts evaluation; obtain baseline height and weight and weigh patients at subsequent visits (p120).":["u6"],"copy|Observe walking and chair rise. Timed Up and Go uses a 3-m walk; over 12 seconds warrants further evaluation (p121).":["u7"],"copy|Cognitive screens can alter the probability of dementia but do not diagnose it; normal results do not completely exclude it (p121).":["u8"],"copy|Ask about depressed mood and anhedonia over 2 weeks; follow a positive brief screen with fuller assessment (p123).":["u9"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; for dementia, caregiver-rated Cornell score at least 6 has 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity (p123).":["u10"],"copy|Distinguish basic self-care, instrumental independent-living tasks and advanced activities; new functional change should prompt diagnostic evaluation (p123).":["u11"],"copy|Combine reported function with observed performance when useful. Function reflects disease in the context of environment and support (p123).":["u12"],"copy|Establish who helps with specific tasks, whether help is paid and who could assist during illness. Financial strain can present as nonadherence, weight loss or apparent neglect (p124).":["u13"],"copy|CGA includes physical health, function, psychological health and socioenvironmental factors (p128).":["u14"],"copy|Its essential sequence is targeting appropriate patients, assessment with recommendations, then implementation and adherence; assessment alone does not ensure benefit (p128).":["u15"],"copy|A positive screen is a starting point for clinical evaluation, not a substitute diagnosis or an automatic drug indication (p117, p121).":["u16"],"exam-cited-book-images|Exam-cited book images":["u17"],"figure-8-2-book-image|Figure 8-2 — book image":["u18"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 8-2, p.124":["u19","u33"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u20"],"copy|2024-Sep Q92":["u21"],"copy|2026-Jun Q38":["u22","u31"],"copy|CHAPTER 8s / HAZZARD":["u23"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 8 - Principles of Geriatric Assessment; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u24"],"copy|Scope of assessment":["u25"],"copy|High-yield screening":["u26"],"copy|Function & support":["u27"],"copy|Management & exam traps":["u28"],"copy|Book images cited by past exams.":["u29"],"copy|Tap an image to zoom.":["u30"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u32"],"copy|Two past-exam questions cite printed pages in Chapter 8.":["u32"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; for dementia, it reports 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity at a Cornell score of at least 6 (p123). In the original Cornell instrument, the clinician rates symptoms using information from the patient and an informant. Alexopoulos et al., 1988.":["u10"],"copy|Levels 7–9. At 7, the person depends on others for personal care but can be relatively stable. At 8, complete dependence accompanies approaching the end of life, with little capacity to recover from even a minor illness. Level 9 describes an expected survival under six months in someone not otherwise living with severe frailty (Figure 8-2, p124). Terminal illness and extreme functional dependence answer different questions. Hazzard 8e, pp. 123–124.":["u42"],"copy|Observe walking and chair rise. Timed Up and Go uses a 3-m walk; over 12 seconds warrants further evaluation in the prose; Table 8-3 reports a >15-second test cutpoint (p121).":["u7"],"copy|The chapter reports PHQ-9 above 10 with 88% sensitivity and 88% specificity; the chapter says caregivers can complete the Cornell scale in dementia and reports 93% sensitivity and 97% specificity at a Cornell score of at least 6 (p123). Current practice (not the book): In the original Cornell instrument, the clinician rates symptoms using information from the patient and an informant. Alexopoulos et al., 1988.":["u10"],"copy|Table 8-3 on the same page reports test performance using a different cutpoint, over 15 seconds.":["u197"],"copy|128–131":["u198"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ASCO recommends geriatric assessment to identify vulnerabilities in older adults receiving systemic cancer therapy.":["u199"],"copy|ASCO 2023 guideline.":["u120"]},"maxId":199,"sequenceOffsets":[[200,-166],[208,-165],[285,-164],[361,0]]}
